# STEP 2: Build SMS notifications for the full Procurement Lifecycle (MR + PR)

You already completed the Step 1 audit. I confirm it. Now build the solution.
Do NOT redesign the audit. Implement it, following the rules and the flow below.

## 0. Core rule
Every time an MR or PR changes status, or is handed to a new owner,
the NEW action owner must receive an SMS IMMEDIATELY (within seconds).
Rejections, returns and cancellations must also notify the right people.
No status change may be silent.

## 1. Fix the global blocks first
- Remove or neutralize the global block from commit 075df0c.
  Set PROCUREMENT_SMS_ENABLED=true in .env and .env.example.
- ProcurementSmsService::send() must no longer abort when
  config('procurement_handoffs.enabled') is false for status-change SMS.
  Keep the flag only as an admin master switch, default ON.
- Restore the controller triggers stripped in 075df0c
  (mr_submitted, planning_forwarded, planning_rejected, coordinator_forwarded),
  but do it through the central hook below, not by copy-pasting into controllers.

## 2. Architecture (as proposed in the audit)
1. Central Transition Hook: ONE dispatcher (e.g. ProcurementTransitionNotifier)
   called from model observers / DB::afterCommit, so every status change
   triggers it automatically, from controllers and from ProcurementLifecycleService.
2. Central Status-to-Owner Map (config file or dedicated class) covering
   every MR and PR status and transition, including side paths.
3. Recipient Priority Resolution:
   1. Direct person (assigned_finance_staff_id, driver_employee_id,
      requested_by, assigned coordinator)
   2. Project / store staff (store manager of the target store,
      coordinator assigned to the project)
   3. Role match (planning, coordinator, store_manager, purchase_manager,
      purchase, general_service, gm, finance_head, store_keeper)
   4. Global admin fallback, only if nobody is found. Log a warning.
4. Deduplication guard: key = model + id + status + recipient_phone, 60s TTL.
5. Non-blocking: send after commit, 5s timeout, try/catch.
   A gateway failure must NEVER roll back or crash the user's action.
6. Logging: every attempt (sent / failed / skipped, with the reason)
   goes to procurement_sms_logs.
7. Skip and log (do not crash) if a recipient has no phone number.

## 3. Message format
"{MR/PR} #{number} ({site/project}): {what happened} by {actor name}.
Action needed: {action}. Open: {link}"
- Emergency requests: prefix "[URGENT-EMERGENCY] ".
- FYI messages say "FYI:" instead of "Action needed:".
- Rejection messages must include the rejection reason (truncated to ~80 chars).
- Keep under 160 characters where possible. Otherwise shorten the reason first.

## 4. Required flow (MR = Material Request)

| # | Event | Who acts | SMS goes to | Message meaning |
|---|-------|----------|-------------|-----------------|
| 1 | Site Engineer creates MR (normal or EMERGENCY) | Site Engineer | Planning Manager (role: planning) | New MR waiting for planning approval. Emergency = [URGENT-EMERGENCY] and sent immediately |
| 2 | Planning APPROVES | Planning Manager | The Coordinator assigned to this project (role: coordinator) | MR approved, forward to store |
| 3 | Planning REJECTS | Planning Manager | Site Engineer (requester) | MR rejected + reason |
| 4 | Planning says "Buy with petty cash from site" | Planning Manager | Site Engineer (requester) | Approved for site petty-cash purchase, go buy it |
| 5 | Coordinator SENDS TO STORE MANAGER | Coordinator | Store Manager of the target store | MR waiting for stock check |
| 6 | Store Manager REJECTS | Store Manager | Planning Manager (+ FYI to Site Engineer) | MR rejected by store + reason |
| 7 | Store Manager says "Send to Purchase" (out of stock) | Store Manager | Procurement Manager (purchase_manager) | MR converted to PR, needs procurement decision |
| 8 | Store Manager says "Create Transfer" (SIV issued) | Store Manager | General Service (general_service) to assign a driver, plus FYI to Site Engineer and Coordinator | Transfer needs a driver |
| 9 | Procurement Manager chooses an option (Buy via Proforma / Direct Buy) and sends to PR Team | Procurement Manager | Procurement Team (role: purchase) | PR assigned, start proforma / direct buy |
| 10 | Procurement Manager SENDS BACK to Store Manager | Procurement Manager | Store Manager | PR returned, with reason |
| 11 | MR deleted / cancelled | Requester or admin | Current owner (FYI) | MR cancelled |

## 5. Required flow (PR = Purchase Request), continue the same logic
Use rows 8-35 of your audit table as the source of truth.
For EVERY row, wire the SMS to the "Current Owner After Change" column. Specifically:
- PR submitted -> Planning
- Planning approves -> Coordinator
- Coordinator approves -> Store Manager
- Store sends to procurement -> Procurement Manager
- Procurement Manager sends to team -> Procurement Team
- Direct Buy / Proforma submitted -> Procurement Manager
- Variance / proformas sent to GM -> GM
- GM approves cash -> Finance Head. GM approves credit -> Finance Head.
- GM counters -> Procurement Manager. GM rejects -> Requester + Procurement Manager.
- Finance Head assigns payment -> the DIRECTLY assigned finance staff
  (assigned_finance_staff_id), NOT a general pool.
- Payment done -> Purchaser (receipt upload needed)
- Receipt uploaded -> General Service / Fleet Manager. Receipt rejected -> Purchaser.
- Driver booked -> the DIRECTLY assigned driver (driver_employee_id) + Store Keeper.
- Driver delivered -> Site Store Keeper
- GRN / intake complete -> Finance Head
- 3-way match closed (completed) -> Requester + Procurement Manager (FYI)
- Emergency direct to finance -> Finance Head, marked URGENT
- Selective transfer -> Store Keeper + Requester
- Cancelled -> Requester + current owner (FYI)
- Reactivated from rejected -> Procurement Manager
- Finance returns to GM -> GM. PM returns to Store -> Store Manager.

## 6. Admin visibility
Settings page that shows:
- Each status, its owner role, and a toggle to enable/disable SMS for that status.
- A Failed SMS log table (time, record, recipient, error) with a "Retry" button.
- A "Test SMS" button.

## 7. Acceptance tests (write them, make them pass)
1. Site Engineer creates an emergency MR -> Planning Manager gets an SMS
   with [URGENT-EMERGENCY] within seconds.
2. Planning approves -> only the project's Coordinator gets an SMS.
3. Planning rejects -> only the Site Engineer gets an SMS with the reason.
4. Planning "petty cash" -> Site Engineer gets an SMS.
5. Coordinator sends to store -> only that target store's Store Manager gets an SMS.
6. Store Manager rejects -> Planning Manager gets an SMS.
7. Store Manager "send to purchase" -> Procurement Manager gets an SMS.
8. Store Manager "create transfer" -> General Service gets an SMS.
9. Procurement Manager sends to team -> Procurement Team gets an SMS.
10. Procurement Manager sends back -> Store Manager gets an SMS.
11. Calling the same transition twice within 60s sends only ONE SMS per recipient.
12. If the SMS gateway is down, the user action still succeeds and the failure is logged.
13. If an assigned person has no phone, it is logged as "skipped" and the action still succeeds.
14. Every status in the audit table (rows 1-35) has a mapped owner. Add a test
    that fails if any status has no mapping.

## 8. Deliverables
- Code, config, migration (if the logs table needs changes), and tests.
- Then give me a final table: Status | Who gets SMS | Message text | Test passed.
- List any status where the recipient could not be determined, and what fallback was used.
- Do not touch unrelated features. Keep the change reviewable (small commits).